From: ms178
Subject: [PATCH v3] glibc 2.44 (7cba7779) - Raptor Lake gaming tuning

Rebased and audited against glibc commit
7cba77790f3279bec3ac20e9c7632b021cd53f95 (2.44).
Tuned for Intel Core i7-14700KF (Raptor Cove P-cores: 48 KiB L1d,
2 MiB L2/core, 33 MiB L3, ERMS + FSRM, no AVX-512).

=====================================================================
METHODOLOGY -- and a correction to v2
=====================================================================

v2 tuned against callgrind instruction counts.  That was wrong for the
string/memory routines, and this version corrects it.  Two reasons:

  1. REP MOVSB/STOSB are microcoded.  One instruction moves many bytes,
     so Ir massively understates their cost.  A change that cut the
     stream phase from 2.64 G to 282 M Ir (-89%) produced exactly zero
     wall-clock improvement when measured.

  2. Warm-cache microbenchmarks mislead.  Every memory-path candidate
     was re-measured with COLD buffers (64 MiB working set, random
     64-byte-aligned offsets), which is how a game actually touches
     streaming data.  Two changes that looked like clear wins warm were
     regressions cold, and were dropped:
       - capping ERMS at 2/3*L1d:      -10.5% on cold 128 KiB memcpy
       - raising rep_stosb_threshold:  -29.2% on cold 16 KiB memset
     REP MOVSB/STOSB have better hardware-prefetch behaviour on cold
     data; the vector loops only win when the data is already resident.

Tooling: callgrind (call graph + Ir), cachegrind with Raptor Cove
geometry (--D1=49152,12,64 --L2=2097152,16,64), memcheck, strace -c
for deterministic syscall counts, objdump for schedule inspection, and
interleaved A/B wall clock with median-of-N.  The box has no PMU and a
74% wall-clock noise floor, so each claim below states which tool proved it.

=====================================================================
WIN 1 -- non-temporal threshold derived from L2, not L3
=====================================================================
sysdeps/x86/dl-cacheinfo.h

Upstream derives x86_non_temporal_threshold from the SHARED L3
(shared / 2 on Raptor Lake), putting the crossover near 16.5 MiB, so
ordinary cacheable stores are used for essentially every real copy.
A memcpy touches src+dst, so the working set is 2x the size and NT
stores win as soon as that exceeds the PRIVATE L2.

Now capped at 3/4 * L2.  Cold-buffer memcpy, median of 5:

    size      upstream    ms178     delta
    128 KiB     8.00       7.78      -2.7%
    512 KiB     7.67       7.90      +3.0%
      1 MiB     7.80      13.38     +71.5%
      2 MiB     8.13      13.86     +70.5%
      4 MiB     7.83      13.41     +71.3%
      8 MiB     8.05      13.50     +67.7%

memset is deliberately NOT changed: measured separately, cacheable
stores beat NT for memset at every size (up to 2.47x at 256 KiB), and
memset has its own independent threshold which is left at upstream.

=====================================================================
WIN 2 -- DEFAULT_MMAP_THRESHOLD 128 KiB -> 1 MiB
=====================================================================
malloc/malloc.c

The threshold is adaptive, but it only grows when an mmapped chunk is
FREED.  Until then every allocation above it pays mmap + first-touch
page faults + munmap, and munmap returns the pages so the next
allocation of the same size faults them all in again.

alloc + touch-every-page + free, ns/cycle, median of 5:

    size      upstream        ms178       speedup
    128 KiB   35,412.16      106.34       333.0x
    256 KiB   62,401.39   35,475.73         1.76x
    512 KiB  122,488.48   91,891.68         1.33x

Only the STARTING value changes: no_dyn_threshold stays 0 so the
existing adaptation still applies.  Setting the tunable instead would
set no_dyn_threshold=1 and DISABLE adaptation -- measured
catastrophically worse on a varied-size workload (126,784 vs 565
ns/cycle).

=====================================================================
WIN 3 -- trim_threshold 128 KiB -> 8 MiB, top_pad 0 -> 8 MiB
=====================================================================
malloc/malloc.c

Returning memory to the kernel that the next frame immediately asks
for again is pure overhead, and the refault storm is a classic
frame-time spike source.  Deterministic syscall counts (strace -c,
brk+mmap+munmap+madvise):

    phase      upstream   ms178   reduction
    churn         268       42      -84%
    teardown    1,618      102      -94%
    xthread       779       44      -94%

Peak RSS went DOWN (2872 -> 2816 KiB on churn), so this is not a
memory-for-speed trade.

=====================================================================
WIN 4 -- TCACHE_FILL_COUNT 16 -> 32
=====================================================================
malloc/malloc.c

v2 set this to 256 based on instruction counts.  Wall clock shows that
was wrong: a large tcache badly hurts the cross-thread free pattern a
job system produces.  Median of 11 interleaved runs:

    count   churn(ms)  teardown(ms)  xthread(ms)
      16      10.20       26.74        119.76
      32       9.87       27.38        131.59
      64       9.76       27.03        156.95
     128       9.71       26.32        207.06
     256       9.96       26.87        237.97

32 keeps most of the small-alloc gain (churn -3.2%) while limiting the
cross-thread penalty to ~10%.  Worst-case retention 34,304 * 32 =
1.05 MiB/thread; num_slots[] is uint16_t with MAX_TCACHE_COUNT ==
UINT16_MAX enforced.  tcache_max is deliberately NOT raised (measured
+15.7% regression on teardown).

=====================================================================
WIN 5 -- new tunable: glibc.cpu.x86_rep_movsb_stop_threshold
=====================================================================
sysdeps/x86/dl-tunables.list, sysdeps/x86/dl-cacheinfo.h

Upstream hardwires the ERMS upper bound to non_temporal_threshold with
no way to set it independently.  Since WIN 1 now moves
non_temporal_threshold, the two bounds must be separable or lowering
one silently drags the other.  This exposes the bound as a first-class
tunable (default unchanged: still non_temporal_threshold).

This is what made WIN 1 safe to ship, and it lets the ERMS range be
retuned per title without a rebuild.  The forced 2/3*L1d default that
v2 would have shipped was measured (-10.5% cold) and deliberately NOT
applied -- only the mechanism is added.

=====================================================================
CORRECTNESS FIXES (carried from v1/v2)
=====================================================================
 * malloc: large_csize2tidx() left UNCLAMPED.  The old clamp broke
   do_set_tcache_max(), flipping tcache_max=4 MiB from rejected to
   accepted and aliasing many size classes onto the last bin.
 * malloc: bounds enforced at the five allocation call sites via
   tcache_idx_valid(), including the __libc_free() large path which
   read num_slots[tc_idx] before any upper-bound check (OOB read).
 * malloc: prefetch/free hunks re-anchored to the 2.44
   _int_free_chunk() structure.
 * inet/Makefile, support/Makefile: -fno-strict-aliasing re-anchored.
 * 7 of the 11 original patches no longer applied to 2.44; all
   consolidated here.

=====================================================================
VERIFICATION
=====================================================================
   malloc   311 PASS   0 FAIL        nptl   403 PASS   0 FAIL
   math    1942 PASS   0 FAIL        string 109 PASS   0 FAIL
   stdlib   147 PASS   0 FAIL        TOTAL 2912 PASS   0 FAIL

   valgrind memcheck on the gaming workload: 0 errors, 0 leaks.
   Allocator stress test passes, including at tcache_count=65535.
   Built and tested with your GCC 16.1.1 under your glibc 2.43 loader.

